<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml">

    <!--
        THE NAMING RULE — read this before adding or renaming any token.

        Settled 2026-08-07. Two orderings had drifted into this file
        (DmSurfaceTavern category-first, DmNeutralSurface role-first) and the obvious
        fix — "category-first everywhere" — was tried on paper and REJECTED, because it
        produces DmTextConfirm and DmSurfaceScrollbarThumb, which are worse than the
        names they replace. The orderings are not drift. They are TWO FAMILIES, and
        each is internally coherent:

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        FAMILY 1 — STRUCTURAL.  Dm<Category><SurfaceFamily><Variant>

          The shared surfaces every screen is built out of. The qualifier is the
          SURFACE FAMILY, so the category leads and the family groups.

            Category      Surface | Border | Text
            SurfaceFamily Tavern, Parchment, Popup, ToolPanel, ToolChip, ComboItem,
                          Stepper, Notebook, TextSelection
            Variant       Raised, Deep, Muted, Hover, Pressed, Active, Disabled

            DmSurfaceTavernRaisedHover   DmBorderParchment   DmTextComboItemDisabled

          Sorting a structural token by its own name puts it next to its family. That
          is the whole benefit and it is why the category leads.

          DmTextPlaceholder is the one structural token whose qualifier is a text ROLE
          rather than a surface family, and it is registered as such in the test.

        FAMILY 2 — SEMANTIC.  Dm<Role>[<SurfaceFamily>][<Category>][<Variant>]

          A named action role, or a named feature part that is drawn rather than
          inhabited. The ROLE is the identity, so the role leads. A category suffix
          appears only where the role owns more than one colour.

            Role          Confirm, Destructive, Save, Discard, Neutral, AccentGold,
                          Gold, Ribbon, Banner, Map, DragPlaceholder, AreaSelected,
                          EditorGlow, Scrollbar, PopupButton, Hairline, SavedStamp
            Category      Surface | Border | Stroke | Fill | Tint | Text

            DmConfirm  DmConfirmText  DmDestructive  DmDestructiveOnPopup
            DmMapAreaStroke  DmMapAreaFillActive  DmDragPlaceholderFill

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        THE TWO CLAUSES THAT MAKE IT DECIDABLE

        (a) A STRUCTURAL token's name begins with Surface, Border or Text. A SEMANTIC
            token's name does NOT. That is the mechanical half, and
            TokenNamingTests.EveryColourTokenObeysTheNamingRule reads it.

        (b) A token STATES THE CATEGORY IT PAINTS unless it names a drawn element that
            has exactly one paintable part. DmMapGrid, DmRibbonRead, DmScrollbarThumb
            and DmBannerEdge each paint one thing and need no category word; DmMapArea
            did not, which is why it is now DmMapAreaStroke — its own siblings already
            spelled Fill and Stroke.

        A role hue with NO category — DmDestructive, DmDestructiveOnPopup, DmConfirm —
        is deliberate, not an omission: a role hue may paint a fill, a label or a
        stroke as the site requires, and DmDestructive does all three today. Its
        CATEGORISED DERIVATIVES are the ones that carry a category word
        (DmDestructiveText, DmDestructiveHoverSurface).

        On<SurfaceFamily> is the sanctioned way to say "this role hue as rendered on a
        named surface". It has exactly one instance today, DmDestructiveOnPopup, and
        is preferred over an adjective: the rejected DmDestructiveBright named the
        symptom (it is lighter) rather than the reason (the popup is near-black).

        STYLE KEYS TAKE A "Style" SUFFIX IF AND ONLY IF A COLOUR OF THE SAME NAME
        EXISTS. §5 of the design spec called DmBorderTavernStyle "the only style key
        with a Style suffix"; that was wrong — DmBorderParchmentStyle has one too, and
        those two are exactly the two style keys that collide with a colour token
        (DmBorderTavern, DmBorderParchment). No other style key collides and none
        carries the suffix. The suffix is disambiguation, not decoration, and
        TokenNamingTests.StyleKeysCarryTheStyleSuffixExactlyWhenTheyCollideWithAColour
        holds the rule in both directions.

        THE STANDING EXCEPTIONS, each with its reason, are listed in that same test
        rather than here, so that a token cannot be exempted without the exemption
        being read by something.
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

        SINGLE-LAYER DECISION — read this before adding tokens (Tasks 4-11).

        This file was planned as two dictionaries: Palette.xaml holding raw named
        colours, and Tokens.xaml aliasing them under semantic names by putting a
        StaticResource reference in the element content of a Color entry.

        That form does not compile. A markup extension in element-content position
        is handed to ColorTypeConverter as a literal string, and the MAUI XAML
        source generator rejects it with one error per aliased entry:

            Resources/Theme/Tokens.xaml(1,2): error MAUIG1010:
            Cannot convert "... DmPaletteInk900" into "Microsoft.Maui.Graphics.Color".

        Verified by spike on 2026-08-05 against net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0.
        A StaticResource reference still works in ATTRIBUTE position, which is why
        the SolidColorBrush entries below alias their colours successfully.

        So the palette layer is merged into this file. This is now the ONLY file
        in the application permitted to contain colour literals; ThemeDictionaryTests
        enforces that. Add new colours here as semantic names, never as a raw hex
        at a usage site.

        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        COLOURS SHARED ACROSS TOKENS — CHANGE A WHOLE HUE AT ONCE

        The abandoned two-layer design encoded this structurally: each of these
        tokens was a reference to one palette entry, so a restyle edited one line.
        In a single layer they are separate lines that must still move together,
        and only this table says so.

        GROUPED BY BASE RGB, IGNORING ALPHA — and that is the point. An earlier
        revision of this ledger was built by exact string equality, so it saw only
        character-identical literals and listed 5 of the 12 tokens carrying the
        gold. Restyling the gold by editing those 5 lines would have left seven
        tinted derivatives sitting on the old hue. THE ALPHA DERIVATIVES ARE PART
        OF THE RAMP AND MUST MOVE WITH THEIR BASE.

          #C8A040 — gold accent (12 tokens)
            (opaque) DmAccentGoldLight, DmDragPlaceholderStroke,
                     DmAreaSelectedBorder, DmMapAreaStroke, DmMapObjectPlaceholder
            10       DmGoldHoverTint
            18       DmAreaSelectedTint
            20       DmDragPlaceholderFill
            33       DmPopupButtonHover
            40       DmMapAreaFillActive
            66       DmPopupButtonPressed
            90       DmMapAreaStrokeActive

          #8B5E1A — parchment border / tavern raised wash (6 tokens)
            (opaque) DmBorderParchment
            2E       DmSurfaceTavernRaised
            47       DmHairline
            52       DmSurfaceTavernRaisedHover
            6A       DmSurfaceTavernRaisedActive
            6B       DmHairlineStrong

          #C4862A — accent gold, darker (5 tokens)
            (opaque) DmAccentGold
            08       DmEditorGlowTint
            1A       DmMapGrid
            4D       DmBorderTavern
            A6       DmBorderTavernHover

          #CC3030 — destructive on the near-black popup (3 tokens)
            (opaque) DmDestructiveOnPopup
            33       DmPopupButtonDestructiveHover
            66       DmPopupButtonDestructivePressed

          #8B2020 — destructive on parchment (2 tokens)
            (opaque) DmDestructive
            24       DmDestructiveHoverSurface

          #7A5030 — muted parchment ink (2 tokens)
            (opaque) DmTextParchmentMuted
            8C       DmTextPlaceholder

          #F5E4B0 — tavern text (2 tokens)
            (opaque) DmTextTavern, DmMapVertex

          #4A3820 — WinUI scrollbar and selection wash (2 tokens)
            66       DmScrollbarThumb, DmSurfaceTextSelection

        DmSurfaceTavernRaisedActive #6A8B5E1A and DmHairlineStrong #6B8B5E1A share
        a base but differ by one in the alpha channel — see the NEAR-DUPLICATE note
        further down; they are still one hue for restyle purposes.

        Keep this table correct when adding tokens, and check it by BASE RGB rather
        than by whole literal: a base that appears at two keys and is not grouped
        here is a latent restyle bug. All literals in this file are uppercase so
        that check stays a plain case-sensitive search.
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    -->

