    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Feature-specific: hairlines, placeholders and notebook state

         THE THREE RULES — ONE ROLE, THREE VALUES, AND THE VALUES ARE LOAD-BEARING.
         DmDivider used to sit six sections up, in a second vocabulary, for the same
         job these two do. The naming pass asked whether the three are one concept and
         the answer is NO — they are one ROLE (a 1px separating rule) carried by three
         colours that cannot substitute for one another:

         Consumers are cited by grep anchor, not by line number. Every line number this
         comment block once carried had gone stale by the next commit; the anchors below
         are x:Names and Text values that resolve uniquely and survive an edit above them.

           DmHairline       #478B5E1A  28% of DmBorderParchment. Horizontal rules: the
                                       notebook footer rule in NotebookView.xaml, and
                                       the BoxView x:Name="AreaSeparator" in
                                       FloorSidebarItem.xaml.
           DmHairlineStrong #6B8B5E1A  42% of the same base. One consumer, the heavier
                                       rule under the notebook header — the sole
                                       DmHairlineStrong reference in NotebookView.xaml.
           DmHairlineSolid  #3A2E1A    OPAQUE, and off that ramp entirely. Vertical
                                       rules between icon-button groups:
                                       ControlsPaletteView.xaml (on the #EDD8A0
                                       parchment tool panel) and DungeonDetailPage.xaml
                                       twice (on the #1A1610 popup).

         So they were NOT collapsed. The first two are alpha washes of the parchment
         border and sink into whatever they lie on — they are listed under #8B5E1A in
         the shared-colour table at the top and must move with it. The third is opaque
         precisely BECAUSE it has to read on two surfaces at opposite ends of the value
         range: a 28% wash of #8B5E1A over #EDD8A0 parchment is nearly invisible, and
         over #1A1610 it is a dark ghost. Merging any pair would change rendered pixels,
         which is a restyle decision and not this pass's to make.

         What WAS wrong was the vocabulary. "Divider" named a colour after a component
         — and after a component that exists: Controls/DmDivider is a ContentView, so
         the token and the type shared a name. "Hairline" is already this theme's word
         for a 1px rule (see DmStrokeHairline, the thickness), two of the three tokens
         already used it, and "Solid" states the axis that actually varies rather than
         implying a third weight step between Hairline and HairlineStrong.

         DmSavedStampText is deliberately not DmConfirm: the source note in
         NotebookView.xaml.cs above "private static Color SavedColor" says "committed"
         must read as reassuring rather than as an action to take. It gained its Text
         suffix in the naming pass — it sets SaveStateLabel.TextColor and nothing else,
         and its action-role neighbours (DmConfirmText, DmDestructiveText, DmRibbonText)
         all spell it. Bare "SavedStamp" invited it to be read as a badge fill.
    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <Color x:Key="DmHairline">#478B5E1A</Color>
    <Color x:Key="DmHairlineStrong">#6B8B5E1A</Color>
    <Color x:Key="DmHairlineSolid">#3A2E1A</Color>
    <Color x:Key="DmTextPlaceholder">#8C7A5030</Color>
    <Color x:Key="DmDestructiveHoverSurface">#248B2020</Color>
    <Color x:Key="DmSavedStampText">#4A6A2A</Color>

